The document goes on to describe Bluetooth interaction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Putting the device into station mode
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A Pico can act either as a client in station mode (in which case it connects to a network) or as an access point (in which case it generates a network to which other devices connect).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We will consider each,
 but let us start with station mode in which the Pico W will connect to a network.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We enable station mode by way of the pico_cyw43_arch library function cy243_arch_enable_sta_mode ().
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Connecting to the network
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
After having initialized the hardware and putting it into station mode we can make a connection to an access point.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are three types of connection:
 blocking,
 timout and async.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As the names suggest,
 a blocking connection will block until a connection has been made,
 timeout will block until a specified time has passed and async will attempt an asynchronous connection in a non-blocking fashion.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Furthermore,
 each of these three types of connection comes in two versions,
 one that includes a MAC address and one that does not.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
All options are enumerated below.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note that the auth argument represents the security protocol.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Macros for all options are available in pico-sdk/lib/cyw43-driver/src/cyw43_ll.h.
\end_layout

